Syntax: forge <slot> <race> <ingot> <tier>
        forge weapon <kind> <ingot>
        forge hone <weapon>
        forge combine <ingot> <ingot>
        forge resize <item> <race>
        forge refit <item> <slot>

Forge is six kinds of work at one anvil.

FORGE <slot> <race> <ingot> <tier> beats out a piece of armor in light,
medium or heavy, sized to the race you name.  Hardness caps the tier and
volume decides how much metal it takes, so a bar of mithril goes further
than a bar of iron of the same weight.

FORGE WEAPON <kind> <ingot> makes any weapon you know how to make.  The
finer the kind, the more skill it asks: a pickaxe is open to anyone, a
stiletto to very few.  What comes off the anvil strikes at six sevenths of
your level and stops counting at level thirty-five.

FORGE HONE <weapon> is the only way past that.  Ten landed strokes buy a
point of damage, up to your own level plus a point for every twenty of the
skill you know.  Hard metal resists being made finer.

FORGE COMBINE <ingot> <ingot> folds the second bar into the first, making
one bigger bar.  Both must be the same metal and the second must carry no
stats.  The grade of the result is the two averaged by size, and it never
comes out worse than the first bar was.

FORGE RESIZE <item> <race> reworks a finished piece to another body.  Making
one bigger needs a bar of its own metal; making one smaller leaves an
offcut carrying everything the piece carried but its armor.

FORGE REFIT <item> <slot> moves a piece onto a different part of the body
entirely -- a bracer becomes a greave.  The body it was cut for does not
change: a gnome's bracer becomes a gnome's greave.

The armor it offers is conserved across the move.  A slot that carries more
of a suit's protection needs a lower level to offer the same defence, so the
piece keeps what it was worth rather than gaining by the move.  Packing a
large piece into a small slot loses whatever will not fit; it does not
concentrate.

Growing into a larger slot needs metal for the difference, and shrinking into
a smaller one leaves an offcut, exactly as resizing does.

When you are making something -- a piece, a weapon -- every miss costs the
work: a point off the projection, a point off one of the stats being carried,
and metal off the bar it came from.  A poor bar misses more and punishes each
miss harder.

Reshaping is different.  Resizing and refitting degrade nothing; a miss there
costs you only the time and the sweat.

